00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:04.200
Abraham: Cyber security frameworks
are a system of standards, guidelines

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.520
for best practice to manage and
mitigate cybersecurity risks.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.340
Nicole: There are many different
types of frameworks according to the

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.459
specific areas that they need to cover.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:18.130
Following an appropriate cyber security
framework will help to manage an

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:22.450
organisations risk, as long as it
takes into consideration specific

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:26.669
needs and priorities of the different
types of businesses and organisations.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.259
Abraham: There are three
broad types of cyber security

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:31.830
frameworks these consists of

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.129
• Control frameworks- these are
often used by those who are new

00:00:36.129 --> 00:00:40.430
to cyber security, these offer a
basic set of controls to follow.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:46.230
• Program frameworks -these assess the
overall state of a security program

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.700
and help to build and analyse a system
conducting industry comparisons.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:56.050
• Risk frameworks- these focus on
risk and prioritise security.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:01.440
Nicole: Defining and communicating
an organisation’s risk regime is

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.810
key to their overall cyber security.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:09.100
The NCSC recommends implementing
10 key steps to help to protect

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.599
a business from cyber attack.

00:01:11.209 --> 00:01:13.209
Let’s have a look at what is involved.

00:01:16.719 --> 00:01:19.570
Abraham: Step 1 is setting
up a risk management regime.

00:01:20.059 --> 00:01:22.600
This should be embedded
across an organization and

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.270
supported by senior management.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.879
This can involve conducting an
inventory of all resources and risks.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:34.700
Nicole: Step 2 secure configuration
of all systems should be ensured and

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:39.340
maintained by creating an inventory
and defining a baseline build model.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.620
Also developing a strategy to
remove or disable unnecessary

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.679
functionality from any systems.

00:01:46.199 --> 00:01:48.479
Abraham: Step 3 Network security.

00:01:49.139 --> 00:01:52.570
Networks should be protected from
attack by making sure all connections

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:56.369
in the network are defended for
example by firewalls to filter out

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:00.760
any unauthorized access, including all
cloud services and remote working sites.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.880
Nicole: Step 4 Managing user privileges.

00:02:04.549 --> 00:02:08.789
Effective management processes
should limit user privileges, as

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:12.850
unnecessary system privileges can
give rise to potential misuse.

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:17.559
Sensitive information should only be
accessible for those who need to use it.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:21.269
Abraham: Step 5 User
education and awareness.

00:02:21.849 --> 00:02:26.440
Policies should be in place to provide
acceptable and secure use of systems only.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:32.100
Staff training should make cyber security
a priority to maintain awareness of risks.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:34.820
Nicole: Step 6 Incident management.

00:02:35.349 --> 00:02:40.339
Effective incident management services
will help to maintain resilience and help

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:44.899
potentially reduce any impact to both
the business and stakeholder confidence.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:49.520
Reporting any criminal activity to
law enforcement is also important.

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:52.609
Abraham: Step 7 Malware protection.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:57.709
Developing an appropriate antimalware
policy can help to make staff aware

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:01.960
of how malware can infiltrate a
system via phishing scams etc.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:04.140
Nicole: Step 8 Monitoring.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:09.200
Establishing a monitoring strategy,
can help to detect attempted attacks

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.910
by constantly monitoring all systems
and networks and analyzing logs.

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:17.220
Abraham: Step 9 Removable Media Controls.

00:03:17.619 --> 00:03:21.170
Organisations should create a
policy to control all access to

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.780
removable media and limit its use.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.670
All media should be scanned for
malware before it enters the system.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:32.750
Nicole: Step 10 Mobile and Home
working can expose new risks.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:37.900
It is important to have remote working
policies and train all staff on the use

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.270
of remote access and portable systems.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:45.510
Also making sure all devices have a
baseline security system, can help

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:50.429
to secure systems as can protecting
data in transit and at rest.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:53.340
Now let’s look at the CIS framework.

00:03:53.779 --> 00:03:58.890
The Center for Internet Security [CIS]
has developed a 20-point action plan

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:03.400
that helps organisations to develop
a cyber security framework to prevent

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.260
the most common types of attack.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:08.169
Abraham: This is broken
down into 3 catagories.

00:04:08.639 --> 00:04:13.600
1, Identifying the security environment
using basic controls which covers:

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:18.700
Performing an inventory, documenting
all software assets, checking for

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:22.549
software or hardware that may have
been introduced without permission or

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:28.219
knowledge, checking user permissions,
updating passwords and implementing

00:04:28.219 --> 00:04:30.289
audit logs and other management systems.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:35.370
Nicole: 2, Protecting assets with
foundational controls which covers:

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:39.849
Checking email and browsers are
up to date and protected, malware

00:04:39.849 --> 00:04:44.340
and virus protection, preventing
unauthorized connections, backup

00:04:44.389 --> 00:04:49.640
and recovery plans, secure network
configurations, boundary defences,

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:54.890
data protection measures, additional
access controls, wireless access

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:57.809
controls, account monitoring controls.

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:02.440
Abraham: 3, Developing a security
culture using organizational

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:03.719
controls which covers:

00:05:04.279 --> 00:05:08.100
Implementing security awareness
training, managing the security

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:14.250
lifecycle, developing incident response
procedures, performing penetration tests.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.750
As we have discussed there are many
different types of cyber security

00:05:19.059 --> 00:05:22.919
frameworks, but they all do a similar
job of helping organisations to design

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:24.550
their own cyber security system.

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:29.410
Nicole: It is the cyber security
professional’s role to evaluate

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:32.710
and apply the appropriate
framework for their organization.

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:36.460
Focusing on the business needs
and what elements are the most

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:40.340
important according to the type
of traffic an organization has.

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:44.360
Control frameworks work well for
those organisations that are new

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:48.480
to managing a security program
identifying the current state and

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:50.560
prioritizing future developments.

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:54.989
Abraham: Program frameworks like the
National Institute of Standards and

00:05:54.990 --> 00:06:00.979
Technology [NIST] can help build a mature
program and improve information security.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:06.130
Risk frameworks includes more controls
and management across a wider range

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:09.930
of business operations taking a
systematic approach to risk management.

